Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:07.269 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  anymore  for more than 100 years america's  national parks have dazzled countless  visitors pristine places for future  generations to soak in the country's  rugged beauty but these days the  tourists seem to outnumber the wildlife  in 2019  327 million people  visited our national parks more than  double 50 years ago mark strassman  traveled to utah's zion national park to  brave the crowds while admiring the  beauty  sculpted over millennia zion is an oasis  in southwest utah's desert towering  sandstone cliffs and slot canyons carved  by wind and water a paradise on earth  it's a geological gem like the scale of  the grand canyon and yellowstone's  geysers  timeless natural treasures what teddy  roosevelt called our sacred heritage  but as their popularity grows america's  national parks feel under attack by  americans themselves we've seen the  busiest zaire national park has ever  been is  after copenhagen stan smith the mayor of  springdale utah the gateway community  into zion why is that  look around  even if they're not religious this is  their temple this is their sanctuary  this is where they come to find  peace and solitude and and feel good  there's just a special feeling in zion  but finding that serenity can feel  impossible the issue record crowds at  zion and more than a dozen other  national parks tourists and adventure  seekers drawn by the beauty and driven  outdoors by the pandemic  zion the fourth most visited park in the  system sought 20 thousand people in one  day last month attendance grew more than  60 percent over the last decade park  spokesperson amanda rowland says  planning ahead is critical understand  that you need to go online and make your  reservations for camping understand that  parking is going to be full in the park  so ride that shuttle that's in town and  really be flexible now this is the sort  of view everyone comes to zion looking  for dramatic majestic  timeless the very reason national parks  exist  but take a look down below  see all those cars and rvs snaking  through the canyon  that is the challenge  millions want their zion moment  by daybreak most of the year thousands  of zion visitors have parked their cars  and filed onto shuttles taking them down  the canyon we got to the park at 4 45  this morning caught the first shuttle  out here  got up and down as quick as we could  just to kind of beat all the crowds it's  not just zion where your wilderness  experience can feel compromised arches  national park in utah regularly closes  its entrance by 9 a.m because it's full  at yellowstone old faithful marvels the  masses one million visitors in july  alone a record and look at this image  the great smoky mountains national park  looks theme park packed the pro  which is all these people are enjoying  this incredible beauty but the con is  there's so many people it puts you off  you go wow i mean we have to move aside  or we have to go around people  the crowds have strained nearby  springdale too over the last five years  its 660 full-time residents have felt  stampeded by millions of park lovers  zion national park in springdale are  married we share boundaries but like all  marriages they go through stresses  and this is one of those periods we were  three miles long  and we had parking cars parked on both  sides of the road for three miles and we  called it the metal canyon  [Music]  scott williamson owns zion cycles  springdale's only full-service bike shop  in two years booming demand has tripled  his fleet to 150 bikes business is so  good he's worried for the park the high  season is nine months out of the year  moving towards 12 and moving towards 12.  do you sometimes scratch your head and  say where are all these people coming  from i talk to and have lots of friends  over the last few years that have  stopped coming back because of how  popular it is but the most significant  threat may be to the parks themselves  where the conservation motto is leave no  trace instead people leave trash or  reminders etched in stone of their visit  part of the problem funding congress is  forcing the parks to do more with less  do you worry for the future of the park  concern the park is my business if it  turns into something ugly i lose  business i can't afford to not have the  park  zion's on track to hit nearly 5 million  visitors this year  at 229 square miles it's especially  small and unusual in that 99 percent of  visitors get funneled through a single  canyon the great outdoors quickly can  feel claustrophobic will we reach a  point where there's so much traffic  there's genuine concern for the park  itself  i mean that's a larger discussion about  parks in general right we need to be  asking ourselves what does that look  like what does that mean  important questions for the park service  some have introduced timed entry or  ticket requirements to control crowds  trying to leave the drama to the scenery  for cbs saturday morning i'm mark  strassman at zion national park  i think one great key is there's a lot  of out of the way places in these parks  that you can check out just don't away  from the crowd don't follow the crowd  the good and the bad right

